    <h1>First Trimester Guide: Weeks 1-13</h1>

    <p>The first trimester covers week 1 to the end of week 13. Pregnancy is dated from the first day of your last period, so NHS guidance starts at week 4.</p>

    <h2>What happens in the first trimester</h2>

    <h3>Weeks 1-4: the hidden weeks</h3>
    <p>You may not know you are pregnant for the first four weeks. Your midwife will ask for the date of your last period to work out your due date.</p>

    <h3>Weeks 5-8: early symptoms begin</h3>
    <p>This is when most women first notice pregnancy symptoms:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Missed period</li>
      <li>Breast tenderness</li>
      <li>Nausea, with or without vomiting</li>
      <li>Tiredness</li>
      <li>Needing to pee more often</li>
      <li>Food aversions or cravings</li>
    </ul>
    <p><em>Sources: NHS, Pregnancy symptoms, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/common-symptoms/ (fetched 21 October 2026).</em></p>

    <h3>Weeks 9-13: the first trimester ends</h3>
    <p>By week 12 your baby is about the size of a plum, your waist is likely to thicken, and sickness often eases as the trimester ends.</p>
    <p><em>Sources: NHS, 12 weeks pregnant, https://www.nhs.uk/best-start-in-life/pregnancy/week-by-week-guide-to-pregnancy/1st-trimester/week-12/ (fetched 21 October 2026).</em></p>

    <h2>First-trimester screening</h2>

    <h3>Screening tests in the first trimester</h3>
    <p>The combined test has two parts: a blood sample from your arm between 10 and 14 weeks, and an ultrasound that measures the fluid at the back of your baby's neck at the dating scan. It gives the chance of Down's syndrome, Edwards' syndrome or Patau's syndrome, not a diagnosis. If it was not possible, the quadruple blood test is offered between 14 and 20 weeks. Screening is your choice.</p>
    <p><em>Sources: NHS, Screening for Down's syndrome, Edwards' syndrome and Patau's syndrome, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/your-pregnancy-care/screening-for-downs-edwards-pataus-syndrome/ (fetched 21 October 2026).</em></p>

