    <h2>Common first-trimester symptoms and what helps</h2>

    <h3>Nausea and vomiting</h3>
    <p>Nausea and vomiting, often called morning sickness, are very common in early pregnancy and can happen at any time of day or night. They usually clear up by weeks 16 to 20. A severe form, hyperemesis gravidarum, can cause dehydration and may need hospital treatment.</p>
    <p>NHS advice for milder sickness includes rest, a plain biscuit or dry toast in the morning, small frequent meals of plain low-fat foods, and sipping fluids little and often. Some evidence suggests ginger and acupressure bands may help.</p>
    <p><strong>Contact your clinician:</strong> call your midwife, GP or 111 if you cannot keep food or fluids down for 24 hours, have not peed for more than 8 hours, feel faint when standing, or have tummy pain or a high temperature.</p>

    <h3>Tiredness</h3>
    <p>Feeling tired or exhausted is common in pregnancy, especially in the first 12 weeks. NHS advice is to rest as much as possible and accept offers of help.</p>

    <h3>Other common symptoms</h3>
    <p>The NHS also lists back pain, headaches, indigestion and heartburn, pelvic pain, piles and vaginal discharge as common in pregnancy. A severe headache can be a symptom of pre-eclampsia.</p>
    <p><em>Sources: NHS, Pregnancy symptoms, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/common-symptoms/; NHS, Vomiting and morning sickness, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/common-symptoms/vomiting-and-morning-sickness/; NHS, Tiredness and sleep problems in pregnancy, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/common-symptoms/tiredness/ (fetched 21 October 2026).</em></p>

    <h2>Your appointments and screening timeline</h2>
    <p>You will be offered at least 10 appointments and 2 scans if this is your first baby, and at least 7 if you have given birth before.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Booking appointment, 8 to 12 weeks:</strong> the midwife asks about your health, previous pregnancies, mental health, alcohol and smoking, and checks your blood pressure and urine.</li>
      <li><strong>Blood tests, usually 8 to 12 weeks:</strong> blood group, hepatitis B, HIV and syphilis. Screening for sickle cell and thalassaemia is offered before 10 weeks.</li>
      <li><strong>Dating scan and combined test, 11 to 14 weeks.</strong></li>
      <li><strong>20-week screening scan, offered between 18 and 21 weeks.</strong></li>
    </ul>
    <p>You can contact maternity services at any time if you are worried.</p>
    <p><em>Sources: NHS, Antenatal (pregnancy) care and appointments, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/your-pregnancy-care/your-antenatal-care-and-appointments/; NHS, Screening tests in pregnancy, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/your-pregnancy-care/screening-tests/ (fetched 21 October 2026).</em></p>

    <h2>Foods to avoid and food safety</h2>

    <h3>Cheese, milk and other dairy</h3>
    <p>Avoid unpasteurised milk, cream, yoghurt, butter and ice cream. Soft cheeses with a white rind, such as brie and camembert, and soft blue-veined cheeses must be cooked until steaming hot, because they can contain listeria, which can cause miscarriage or stillbirth.</p>

    <h3>Meat, eggs, fish and alcohol</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Raw or undercooked meat:</strong> avoid it, because there is a small risk of toxoplasmosis, which can cause miscarriage.</li>
      <li><strong>Liver and liver products:</strong> avoid them, because they are high in vitamin A, which can harm your baby.</li>
      <li><strong>Eggs:</strong> hen eggs with the British Lion stamp can be eaten raw or runny. Others should be well cooked.</li>
      <li><strong>Fish:</strong> avoid shark, swordfish and marlin. Limit tuna to 4 medium cans or 2 steaks a week, and oily fish to 2 portions a week.</li>
      <li><strong>Fruit and vegetables:</strong> wash them to remove soil, which can contain toxoplasma.</li>
      <li><strong>Alcohol:</strong> the recommendation is not to drink at all in pregnancy. Drinking increases the risk of miscarriage, premature birth and low birthweight.</li>
      <li><strong>Smoking:</strong> stopping reduces the risk of stillbirth, premature birth and low birth weight.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>If you have already eaten something you should avoid, it is unlikely to affect your baby if you did not become ill.</p>
    <p><em>Sources: NHS, Foods to avoid in pregnancy, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/keeping-well/foods-to-avoid/; NHS, Drinking alcohol while pregnant, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/keeping-well/drinking-alcohol-while-pregnant/; NHS, Stop smoking in pregnancy, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/keeping-well/stop-smoking/ (fetched 21 October 2026).</em></p>

    <AdUnit slot="2263226824" format="auto" />
    <h2>Folic acid and vitamin D</h2>

    <p>Take 400 micrograms of folic acid every day from before you are pregnant until you are 12 weeks pregnant. It helps your baby's nervous system to form and offers some protection from neural tube defects such as spina bifida.</p>
    <p>A higher dose of 5 milligrams a day is advised if you have a higher chance of a pregnancy affected by neural tube defects, for example if you have diabetes or a family history of one. A GP can prescribe it.</p>
    <p>A daily vitamin D supplement of 10 micrograms is also recommended. Do not take cod liver oil or any supplement containing vitamin A (retinol).</p>
    <p><em>Sources: NHS, Pregnancy vitamins and supplements, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/keeping-well/pregnancy-vitamins-and-supplements/ (fetched 21 October 2026).</em></p>

    <h2>Prenatal vitamins and supplements</h2>

    <p>A prenatal vitamin helps fill nutritional gaps during pregnancy. Key nutrients include:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Folic acid:</strong> 400-800 mcg daily, which reduces the risk of neural tube defects</li>
      <li><strong>Iron:</strong> 27 mg daily, to support increased blood volume</li>
      <li><strong>Calcium:</strong> 1,000 mg daily, to support bone development</li>
      <li><strong>Vitamin D:</strong> 600 IU daily, to support bone health and immune function</li>
      <li><strong>DHA:</strong> 200-300 mg daily, to support brain and eye development</li>
    </ul>

<p>Doses differ between countries. NHS guidance sets folic acid at 400 micrograms a day until 12 weeks and vitamin D at 10 micrograms a day. Always discuss supplements with your clinician first.</p>

    <h2>Warning signs that need urgent care</h2>

    <p>Contact your clinician straight away if you have:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Heavy vaginal bleeding, such as soaking a pad in an hour</li>
      <li>Severe abdominal pain or cramping</li>
      <li>A temperature of 38C or above</li>
      <li>Severe vomiting, or you cannot keep liquids down</li>
      <li>Pain or burning when you pee</li>
      <li>A severe headache</li>
      <li>Sudden breathlessness, breathlessness when lying down, chest pain or dizziness</li>
      <li>Fluid leaking from your vagina</li>
      <li>Itchy skin, which can be a sign of a liver condition called intrahepatic cholestasis</li>
    </ul>
    <p><em>Sources: NHS, Pregnancy symptoms you need to get help for, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/common-symptoms/pregnancy-symptoms-you-need-to-get-help-for/ (fetched 21 October 2026).</em></p>

